lines_added: 2200
decisions:
  - id: D-vertical-kanban
    decision: Stacked kanban columns vertically instead of horizontal scroll
    rationale: Sidebar is 320px wide — horizontal kanban columns would be 80px each, unusable. Vertical stacking with section headers reads naturally in a sidebar context.
  - id: D-combine-health
    decision: Used Combine $tasks subscription for AgentHealthMonitor instead of polling
    rationale: TaskStore already publishes changes via @Published. Subscribing via Combine gives instant updates with zero polling overhead. Only recomputes when tasks actually change.
  - id: D-registry-observe
    decision: HotKeyManager.observeRegistry() replaces hardcoded register() calls
    rationale: Makes hotkeys fully dynamic — users can remap without restart. The Combine sink re-registers all bindings on any change. Small risk of brief hotkey gap during re-registration, but CGEvent tap is fast enough that it's imperceptible.
  - id: D-state-machine-bypass
    decision: EyebrowBindingRegistry defaults to useStateMachine=true with opt-out
    rationale: The existing state machine (idle→armed→recording→send) is the right default for voice control. Direct bindings are for power users who want eyebrow raises to trigger arbitrary actions. Defaulting to state machine means zero behavioral change for existing users.
  - id: D-dedup-set
    decision: Added processedNoteIds set to SymphonyMonitor to prevent duplicate TaskStore handling
    rationale: SymphonyMonitor polls every 5 seconds and re-reads full inbox JSONL each time. Without dedup, every task-intent note would be re-handled on every poll cycle, causing duplicate timeline events and incorrect progress.
errors_encountered:
  - description: 'TaskDetailView .frame(width: 450, minHeight: 400) — SwiftUI frame modifier doesn''t accept both width and minHeight in one call'
    resolution: 'Split into two .frame() modifiers: .frame(width: 450).frame(minHeight: 400)'
    time_to_fix: 1min
  - description: TaskStore tests failed due to persisted tasks.json from previous test runs contaminating state
    resolution: Added setUp() that removes ~/.paradigm/conductor/tasks.json before each test
    time_to_fix: 2min
  - description: EyebrowBindingRegistry test failed on testDefaultsEmpty because UserDefaults had stale data
    resolution: Added setUp() that removes the eyebrowBindings and eyebrowUseStateMachine UserDefaults keys
    time_to_fix: 1min
  - description: SentinelWSClient tests tried to mutate private(set) recentEvents directly
    resolution: Rewrote tests to verify public interface (initial state, identifiable conformance, level field) rather than simulating internal buffer behavior
    time_to_fix: 2min
learnings:
  - TaskStore persistence sharing between tests causes cross-contamination — always clean up persisted state in setUp()
  - SentinelWSClient's private(set) on recentEvents means tests can't directly manipulate the buffer — test the public interface instead of internal state
  - 14 parameters in MainOverlayView init is the current cost of explicit ownership threading — acceptable but approaching the threshold where EnvironmentObject would trade clarity for brevity
  - The BindingRegistry pattern (Published dict + CRUD + UserDefaults + actionFromName serialization) appeared twice independently — worth abstracting if a third instance emerges
  - Combine subscription for cross-component reactivity (AgentHealthMonitor ← TaskStore) is cleaner than the timer-based polling pattern used by SymphonyMonitor
