body: |-
  ## Where We Are

  Conductor is at **Sprint 13 / v0.12.0** — 113+ Swift source files, somewhere north of 17K LOC. It started as a floating overlay with a text buffer and gaze tracking. Now it's a genuine multi-agent mission control: you can compose structured tasks, assign them to agent groups, watch progress in a kanban dashboard, see per-agent health sparklines, monitor Sentinel events in real-time, and customize every input binding from eyebrow raises to hotkeys.

  The arc from Sprint 0 (an NSPanel with a waveform icon) to Sprint 13 (health-monitored agent orchestration with live telemetry) is striking. Each sprint layered cleanly on the last. The single-owner pattern in AppDelegate has scaled gracefully — we're now threading 14 distinct state objects through to MainOverlayView, which sounds like it should be unwieldy, but each one has a clear lifecycle owner and the view just observes.

  ## Architectural Observations

  **The TaskStore pattern worked well.** Having SymphonyMonitor route task-intent notes into a dedicated store (rather than bolting tracking onto the monitor itself) kept concerns separated. The monitor polls and indexes; the store interprets and persists. The dedup set (`processedNoteIds`) was a last-minute addition that prevented notes from being re-handled on every poll cycle — easy to miss, catastrophic if forgotten.

  **AgentHealthMonitor via Combine subscription** is the cleanest wiring pattern in the codebase. One `$tasks.sink` and the health view stays perfectly in sync. No polling, no manual refresh. This is how all the cross-component data flow should work eventually — the SymphonyMonitor's 5-second poll loop is the last piece still using a timer.

  **Sprint 13's registry pattern** is worth noting: both EyebrowBindingRegistry and HotKeyBindingRegistry follow the same shape (Published bindings dict, CRUD, UserDefaults persistence, actionFromName/nameFromAction serialization). If we add more input binding types, this could be abstracted into a generic `BindingRegistry<Key: Hashable>`. Not worth doing now with only two instances, but the pattern is established.

  **The HotKeyManager refactor** was the riskiest change — replacing hardcoded `register(.toggleVideo)` calls with `observeRegistry()` means hotkeys are now fully dynamic. Users can remap Cmd+Shift+V to `send` instead of `toggleVideo` without restarting. The Combine observation means adding a binding in the Settings UI immediately takes effect.

  ## What's Missing / What's Next

  **No persistence cleanup.** TaskStore grows unboundedly — there's no archival or pruning of completed tasks. For a desktop app this is fine for months, but eventually we'll want a "clear completed" button or automatic 30-day expiry.

  **SentinelLiveView is passive.** It shows events but can't filter by symbol click, can't jump to code, can't correlate with task timelines. The data is there — SentinelEvent has `symbol` and the TaskStore has `symbolsTouched` — but the cross-referencing UI isn't built yet.

  **BindingsManagerView is getting long.** Five sections in a single Form is manageable but we're approaching the point where each section should be its own view. The shared `actionPicker` ViewBuilder is good — it prevents the 12-option Picker from being duplicated 8 times — but the view itself is ~280 lines.

  **The MainOverlayView init signature is 14 parameters.** This is the cost of the single-owner pattern — everything threads through AppDelegate → launchPanel → MainOverlayView. An EnvironmentObject approach would reduce the parameter threading but would obscure the ownership graph. For now the explicit wiring is the right trade-off: you can read the init and know exactly what data flows where.

  ## Confidence Notes

  High confidence on the model/store layer — TaskStore, AgentHealthMonitor, the registries. These are well-tested (35 tests) and the patterns are proven from ProjectStore and AgentGroupStore.

  Medium-high confidence on the views. They follow established patterns from the codebase (VStack/HStack composition, sheet presentation, DisclosureGroup for collapsibles) but I can't visually verify layout without running the app. The TaskDashboardView's kanban columns are stacked vertically (not horizontal scroll) because the sidebar is narrow — this is the right call for a 320px-wide panel but might feel cramped with many tasks.

  The HotKeyRecorder NSView is the lowest-confidence piece. It works on paper (becomeFirstResponder + keyDown override + NSViewRepresentable), but NSView first responder management in a SwiftUI sheet context can be finicky. May need a `DispatchQueue.main.async { window?.makeFirstResponder(self) }` delay if focus doesn't grab immediately.
confidence: 0.88
